    "markdown": "## Word order in questions: Grammar chart\n\n![Infographic explaining the word order in English questions, with examples for standard question order and questions using the auxiliary verb 'be'. The chart illustrates the placement of question words, auxiliary verbs, subjects, and main verbs or adjectives for forming questions in English.](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-1.png)\n\n#### Questions in general\n\nIf we want to ask a question in English, the order is **QWASM**: **Question word, [**Auxiliary verb**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/auxiliary-verbs-do-be-and-have/), Subject, Main verb**. In **Yes-No questions** (questions where the answer is **yes** or **no**), there is no Question Word.\n\n#### Questions with **be** as the main verb\n\nWhen [**be**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple-forms-of-to-be/) is the main verb, it is used as the **Auxiliary** in the question, and then we don’t have a **Main verb** after the Subject.\n\n#### Auxiliary verbs\n\nIn the position of **Auxiliary**, we can use **be**, **do**, **have** or any modal verb: **[**can**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/can-cant/), could, [**will**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/will-and-shall/), would, [**should**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/should-shouldnt/)**, etc.  \n  \n**Have** is only an auxiliary verb in the form [**have got**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/have-got/) and in the [**present perfect**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/present-perfect/).\n\n-   ***Have** you **got** any brothers or sisters?*\n-   *What **have** you **cooked** for lunch?*\n\nFor other uses of **have**, we need an auxiliary verb (**do**, **did**) for questions.\n\n-   *What time **had you** dinner yesterday? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-2.png)* \n-   *What time **did you have** dinner yesterday? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-3.png)* \n-   ***Have you** to do it now? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-4.png)* \n-   ***Do you** **have** to do it now? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-5.png)* \n\n**Have got** only has a present form. It does not have a past form.\n\n-   ***Had you got** many toys when you were a child? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-6.png)* \n-   ***Did you** **have** many toys when you were a child? ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-7.png)* \n\n## Question words\n\n![Colorful educational chart of question words in English, categorized by their use such as 'WHO' for person, 'WHERE' for place, and 'HOW OLD' for age, with example dialogues for each word](/uploads/grammar/a2/asking-questions-in-english/img-8.png)\n\n#### **How much**\n\nWe can use **how much** to ask about the price of something.\n\n-   ****How much** is the jacket?**\n\n#### **How** + adjective\n\nWe can use **how + adjective** (any adjective) as a question word.\n\n-   ***How tall** are you?*\n-   ***How fast** is your car?*\n\n#### **Which** vs **what**\n\nWe can use **which + noun**, and we can also use **what + noun**. We use **which** when there are a small number of possible answers. Look at the difference:\n\n-   ***Which** car do you like, the red or the blue?* (there is a small number of possible answers)\n-   *‘**What** car have you got?’ ‘A Mercedes.’* (many possible answers)",
